# {{.project_name}}

Skyulf 0.9.1 Bundle: **{{if eq .training_layout "multi_target"}}independent target models{{else}}{{.task}}{{end}}** with **{{.engine}}** training and **{{.inference_mode}}** inference, using **{{.compute_mode}}**
compute. Training and preprocessing fit locally in pandas or Polars. {{if eq .inference_mode "spark"}}Scoring runs a partition-safe MLflow pyfunc on Spark workers and keeps keyed predictions distributed.{{else}}Scoring uses the selected local engine.{{end}}

## Inference mode and capacity

The setup choice is `inference_mode={{.inference_mode}}`. Estimate the rows and
bytes in **each scoring run** and the memory available to the selected compute.
There is no universal row threshold for Spark. Local inference uses the configured
`max_rows` and `max_input_mb` bounds. Spark inference distributes scoring through
`mlflow.pyfunc.spark_udf`; those bounds still apply to pandas training and do not
cap the total scoring population. Keep training samples and budgets sized for the
driver even when scoring runs on many workers.

{{if eq .inference_mode "spark"}}Spark inference requires `engine=pandas` and a fitted artifact whose effective
preprocessing chain is partition safe. Unsupported steps, row-changing behavior,
custom scoring callbacks, temporal history and unsupported model-set composition
fail before publishing predictions. The error identifies the unsupported step;
revise the recipe and retrain, or initialize a local-inference project. Existing
fitted Polars artifacts are not converted automatically.

The first certified set is **SimpleImputer (mean/constant)** and
**StandardScaler**, with **LinearRegression** or **LogisticRegression**, including
their reviewed built-in tuning wrappers. Model sets may combine these independent
components with no custom composition outputs. Other model choices, encoders,
custom callbacks and tuning feature exclusions are not certified by this release.
The model menus cover local inference too; successful Bundle validation does not
certify the fitted artifact for Spark.

`config/workflow.json` records `spark_udf_env_manager={{if eq .compute_mode "serverless"}}local{{else}}virtualenv{{end}}` and
`spark_udf_prediction_batch_rows=10000`. The worker environment must contain
the exact model and Skyulf dependencies recorded with the registered artifact.
Certified packages embed an exact-source Skyulf wheel and its source hash;
{{if eq .compute_mode "serverless"}}serverless `local` uses the declared task environment, including the same
Skyulf wheel and dependency versions on the driver and workers. Verify those
versions and source hash on the selected compute before deployment.{{else}}the job cluster uses `virtualenv` to install that wheel and the recorded
dependency pins in an isolated worker environment. Verify worker startup on the
selected compute before deployment.{{end}} The batch
setting bounds each model prediction call (1–100000 rows), not the total input
or incoming Arrow allocation. Databricks manages Arrow transport; serverless
does not permit changing its Arrow batch configuration. An environment failure
stops scoring without changing the selected manager.
{{end}}

## Model competition for one target

Choose `training_layout=model_competition` to compare named candidates for one
target and select one winner. It uses the `train` and `score` jobs plus the
independent `monitoring` job and
single-model promotion controls. Each candidate has its own training task. Setup asks for the
shared target/task, source, inputs, CV policy, final metric, quality threshold and
lifecycle settings. Choose 2-8 candidates from task-specific model lists, then
choose their common preprocessing recipe and search strategy/settings. Selecting
voting or stacking opens questions for that candidate only: base-model count and
individual model menus, voting weights/type, or stacking meta-model/folds and
passthrough. Classifiers also offer calibration. Each ensemble keeps its own
answers, including when two candidates use the same ensemble type. Select distinct
base models; their automatic search spaces are supplied by Core. Accept defaults
unless you need different settings. Hidden model slots do not enable questions.

The initializer generates `src/modeling/model_competition.py` from those answers; no
Python edits are required to activate the selected models. Default selections:

| Task | Candidate 1 | Candidates 2 and 3 |
| --- | --- | --- |
| regression | Ridge | Random forest and voting (linear regression + Ridge) |
| classification | Logistic regression | Random forest and soft voting (logistic + forest) |

All selected candidates use the shared search controls (default: random,
10 trials), each with its own Core automatic space. Selected ensemble base models
are also tuned automatically. Search CV and the selection metric remain common;
the lifecycle metric chosen during setup also supplies the tuning objective.
Exhaustive grid strategies may exceed the budget with large automatic spaces;
reduce the candidate-specific space or explicitly increase the limits.

Each candidate has its own editable `modeling` object: model params, strategy,
trial controls and a populated `search_space`. Edit one candidate without
changing the others, even when they use the same model type. Recipe functions
stay in `features/preprocessing.py`; select one with `preprocessing_recipe` and
adapt its columns to your input data. Replace the modeling object with a plain
model type and params if you want fixed training without tuning.

New projects have no shared `ensemble.py` or `tuning.py` override files. Older
projects using those hooks still work. Adjust
`competition_max_trials` (setup default 1000) and `competition_max_candidates` (default 8)
in `config/workflow.json` to bound the whole competition. The trial cap covers
the sum of candidate trial upper bounds, including nested searches; a fixed
candidate counts as one per outer/final search. Larger grids must fit those
explicit bounds. This cap counts candidate configurations, not individual model
fits: every configuration uses CV folds, and final fits and CV diagnostics add
work. Input-size limits and the maximum fold count apply separately.

Candidate preprocessing must preserve rows and their order. Put eligibility
filters in the shared pre-split recipe; candidate-specific resampling and sorting
are not supported.
Custom Python is trusted project code and must also preserve row membership and
order; the preflight checks recognize built-in row-changing steps, not arbitrary
Python behavior.

Ordinary tuned CV scores are post-selection diagnostics;
choose Nested CV for independent outer-fold evaluation. The winner alone gets
the reserved holdout quality check. A failed quality check does not fall back
to a runner-up. A failed candidate stops the competition. Start a fresh run
after inspecting failure evidence; lifecycle repairs do not re-register models.
Use the phased Bundle train job for competition; the single-call `run_action`
training adapter does not execute competitions.

For a regression example, choose `task=regression`, your amount target and
`metric=heldout_rmse`. For classification, choose `task=classification`, your class
target and `metric=heldout_f1`. All generated candidates initially use the shared
preprocessing recipe (normally `default`). For example, customize a regression
forest candidate's recipe while retaining its tuning settings:

```python
MODELS["random_forest_regressor_2"]["preprocessing_recipe"] = "example_imputer"
```

`example_imputer` is a named recipe in `src/features/preprocessing.py`; adapt its
`feature_value` column first. Add other named builders there for custom steps or
use `example_imputer_frequency` after adapting its numeric and category inputs. Each candidate
and CV fold fits its selected recipe only on that fold's training rows. Keep
eligibility in the shared `src/features/pre_split.py`. Candidate declarations
cannot override target, task, source, pre-split rules, holdout or CV.

CV is mandatory for competition. The initializer exposes the same fold, group,
time and nested policy controls as single-model training. All candidates share
the same evaluation rows and metric: `heldout_rmse` selects using training-side
RMSE, for example. Candidate ranking never consumes the final holdout. Only the
winner is tested against the holdout and champion gates; a failed gate preserves
the champion without trying a runner-up. Every candidate must complete before
selection. The loader supplies `pipeline.modeling` from each declared candidate
during training.

## Optional multi-target training

The initializer's `training_layout` defaults to `single_model`. Choose
`multi_target` to use the same `train` job with one `train_<name>` task per model; the
project also has a separate `monitoring` job. Setup asks shared data,
output selection, compute and training schedule questions, then 2-8 branches.
For each branch choose its name, target, features, task, model, recipes, split,
CV, tuning and quality settings. Ensemble choices open that branch's base-model
menus and controls. The initializer writes executable `src/modeling/multi_model.py`.
Multi-target keeps branch `promotion_policy=manual_approval` and
`score_handoff=disabled`; whole-set manual/automatic policy is in model_set.py.
The shared `score_handoff` prompt controls the complete set separately. Choose
`after_alias_change` to start the existing score job after successful automatic
promotion, manual approval or rollback. `training_report -> scoring_requested
-> run_batch_scoring` provides this handoff. A pending/rejected candidate or
failed quality check does not trigger scoring. `disabled` keeps scoring manual
or scheduled. Promotion alone does not enable this option.

Edit the generated declarations in `src/modeling/multi_model.py` when needed.
Classification, regression and ensemble branches can be mixed. Each branch has a
`workflow` overlay on
`config/workflow.json` and optional `features_path` relative to `src/modeling`
(default `../features`). Overlay values replace complete top-level fields:
setting `pipeline` replaces the whole pipeline, with no nested model merging.
Keep its preprocessing list empty; the selected Python recipe fills it.
A branch selects `preprocessing_recipe` and `pre_split_recipe` independently.
Omitting a selector calls that phase's default builder. Unknown names fail early.
Use names such as `{catalog}.{metadata_schema}.revenue{resource_suffix}` to
retain deployment bindings and namespace isolation. Feature packages must stay
inside `src`; keep them under `src/features/`, or add their paths to sync includes.
All modeling Python and feature Python files are explicitly included in sync.

For example, keep all custom implementations in the shared `src/features/custom/`
package and select different ordered step lists:

| Branch | preprocessing_recipe | pre_split_recipe |
| --- | --- | --- |
| revenue | example_frequency | example_complete_inputs |
| cost | example_imputer | none |
| churn | example_imputer_frequency | example_complete_inputs |

`preprocessing.py` defines `build_preprocessing(recipe="default")` and
`pre_split.py` defines `build_pre_split_steps(recipe="default")`. Add a named
function to either file's mapping to create a recipe. Each function returns a
normal list of Core/custom steps. The starters use `feature_value` and `category`;
adapt those columns to the selected branch's inputs. `example_frequency` performs
no imputation; `example_imputer_frequency` runs the imputer then custom frequency encoding.
`example_complete_inputs` requires at least one of the two inputs to be present.

Shared recipes share code, not fitted values: each branch and CV fold learns its
own statistics from its own training rows. Selected recipe names are bound into
the saved source, so model reload and saved-plan replay never use another branch's
current/default recipe. Pre-split scoring reuse uses the selected pre-split list.
Legacy zero-argument builders remain valid when selectors are omitted.

All branches share one pinned input Delta version and one captured training
clock. Each has independent labels, preprocessing, optional search/CV, registered
candidate, and comparison against its counterpart in the pinned champion set.
Train-only projects use each model's own champion. Keep every known target
column out of all input feature lists. Missing labels are excluded separately
per branch. The report exposes the parent run and every component result.

`src/modeling/multi_model.py` defines what to train: each target, model, training
and CV settings, preprocessing recipe and quality limits. `src/modeling/model_set.py`
defines how those models are released together: the registered set name, promotion
policy and scoring tables/views. For example, define revenue and cost models in
`multi_model.py`, then use `model_set.py` to approve them as one version and publish
their predictions together. The profit calculation (`revenue - cost`) belongs in
`src/features/scoring.py`.

Multi-target projects enable `src/modeling/model_set.py`. Training packages every
completed component candidate under one model-set name/version/digest. It copies
the frozen component artifacts and shared `src/features/` source into the set.
Component aliases stay unchanged. The set's `promotion_policy` controls its
activation; legacy projects without that factory remain train only.
`src/features/scoring.py` contains both stages: `build_model_rules()` selects each
model's eligibility and output rules; `build_combined_rules()` selects calculations
that use several model results. The latter returns an empty list by default.
To enable one, declare its callback, version, parameters, typed columns and
`required_components` there. The commented profit example depends
only on revenue and cost; an unrelated component's exclusion does not suppress it.
The feature package exports `build_scoring` as the compatible model-rule hook.

### Select published results

The initializer asks `model_set_name`, `model_set_output_mode` and `model_set_table_name` for this
layout. Blank names use project defaults; explicit names use exactly that object
name with the target's catalog/schema and deployment suffix added automatically.
`model_set_name` names the registered model set, not a table (default:
`<project_name>_set`). `model_set_table_name` names its physical result table
(default: `<project_name>_set_scores`). View defaults are
`<project_name>_predictions_<branch>` and `<project_name>_business_results`.
Edit `publication` in `src/modeling/model_set.py` to change the selection:

| Mode | Physical table | Consumer views |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `all` (default) | Model predictions, model outputs and combined business results | None |
| `combined_only` | Combined business results, keys, rule status and set provenance | None |
| `separate_views` | All results saved once | One per selected model, plus combined results when rules exist |

All modes still run each model's saved scoring rules before combined rules.
`combined_only` requires active combined rules in the saved set; configure,
train and approve them first. It omits intermediate predictions from storage.
Switching between this mode and the full schema requires a new table name.

For `separate_views`, setup also asks `model_view_prefix` and
`combined_view_name`. `model_views=None` creates a view for every saved branch;
a mapping selects only its listed branches and gives each a custom name:

```python
"model_views": {
    "revenue": "{catalog}.{output_schema}.revenue_predictions{resource_suffix}",
    "cost": "{catalog}.{output_schema}.cost_predictions{resource_suffix}",
},
"combined_view": "{catalog}.{output_schema}.profit_results{resource_suffix}",
```

`model_views={}` exposes no individual model views. Views contain record keys,
the relevant output/status columns and set provenance; model columns retain their
branch prefix. Views are projections, not additional prediction tables. All read
one committed backing table. A rule error fails the job before any new data is
written; earlier successful results remain. View setup happens before the data
write and can leave empty/old-result views if setup fails. Existing unrelated
objects are never replaced. Changed view sources/projections require new names;
removing a view from configuration does not delete an existing catalog object.

For example, after scoring `id=101`, inserting `id=102` lets the next batch append
its prediction. Updating a feature on `id=101` or deleting that row is different:
the default `source_change_policy="reject"` fails the batch. Choose
`rebuild_on_change` in Bundle setup (or `modeling/model_set.py`) to rebuild the
complete current snapshot with the selected set when UPDATE/DELETE is observed.
Deleted rows disappear, corrected rows and business results are recomputed,
and lag/rolling history starts from the corrected snapshot. Ordinary inserts
still append. The full snapshot must fit `max_rows` and `max_input_mb`; otherwise
the job fails and keeps previous results. Permission failures still fail.
Expired CDF history fails unless `auto_rebuild_on_cdf_expiry=true` enables
recovery of recognized history loss; this is a separate scoring policy.

The two questions have independent purposes: `model_change_mode` controls a
new model-set selection; `source_change_policy` controls changed/deleted source
rows. A source-triggered rebuild uses the selected set for ALL rows, even if
earlier rows were scored with older versions in append mode. There is no
retraining. Old projects without the new setting retain `reject`. This option
currently belongs to model-set scoring; the standalone single-model insert
scorer keeps its existing source-change rejection contract.

### Set quality and activation

Bundle setup asks `model_set_promotion_policy` (default `manual_approval`). Edit
`promotion_policy` in `src/modeling/model_set.py` to choose `automatic` later.
The set policy is separate from individual branch policies, which stay manual.
Set each branch's `metric`, `quality_threshold`, optional `quality_gates` and
`min_improvement` in its `workflow` dictionary in `src/modeling/multi_model.py`.
For example, RMSE with threshold 10 means RMSE <= 10; F1 with threshold 0.8
means F1 >= 0.8. Use limits suitable for your task, including ensemble branches.

The first set needs passing absolute thresholds for every branch. Replacements
require no branch to regress on its selected metric against the corresponding
component in the pinned champion set. At least one branch must improve by its
`min_improvement` (with a positive gain even when the minimum is zero). Equal
peers are allowed; an all-tied set stays challenger. For example, lower revenue
RMSE with unchanged risk accuracy can promote the complete set. All absolute
gates still apply, including additional metrics configured in `quality_gates`.
The quality policy and baseline are saved with the set and evaluated on saved holdout rows.
Editing multi_model.py cannot change a previously registered set's policy.
Branch names and registered component names must match the existing set layout.

Automatic mode checks all gates after packaging. A failed gate leaves the current
champion unchanged and reports the failed components. A complete passing set
becomes champion. Missing evidence, changed baselines and execution errors fail
the job. Scoring stays a separate job. Manual approval checks the same gates.

For manual approval, use the `train` job's `lifecycle_action=approve`, the concrete set
`candidate_version`, and `expected_champion_version` (`none` for first activation).
Approval executes the saved complete set on a bounded scoring-source snapshot and
requires each component and rule to produce at least one result, then verifies
every saved component quality policy before changing the set alias.
Old sets without quality pins remain scoreable but need retraining/packaging for
approval through this Bundle flow.
Save the returned promotion receipt. To restore its prior complete set, use
`lifecycle_action=rollback`, `promotion_receipt_json`, and the expected current
champion version.

Every completed training candidate is nominated as the set's `challenger`. A new
candidate moves the displaced contender to `previous_challenger`. Approval removes
the promoted version's challenger alias and preserves the old champion as
`previous_champion`. The individual component aliases remain unchanged.

Use `lifecycle_action=reject` with the current set `candidate_version`,
`expected_champion_version` and `rejection_reason` to record a manual rejection.
The rejected set stays visible as challenger until a new candidate replaces it;
its `approval_status=rejected` prevents later approval. Rejection does not run
training or scoring, or require passing quality gates. An identical repeat reuses
the original receipt. A changed reason, candidate or champion fails explicitly.
Rollback restores the complete prior set and preserves unrelated challenger history.

The same `score` job calls `score_models.py` for this layout. It pins the set
champion once (or uses `score_model_version`) and publishes the full keyed result
with set identity. Scoring loads saved code, so changing editable rules requires
training/packaging and approving a new set. Both jobs remain serialized with
`max_concurrent_runs=1`; they must exclusively own set aliases and output writes.
Append retains earlier rows with their original set version; full rebuild
atomically replaces compatible output on a set change. Temporal carry changes
require full rebuild. Declare rule dependencies as exact pins in
`src/features/requirements.txt`; conflicting component pins are rejected.
The remaining lifecycle instructions below apply to `training_layout=single_model`.

